WEBVTT

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:04.280
<i>Today we're having a 
barbecue on the riverside</i>

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.380
<i>to welcome the new club members 
and hunt down rocks!</i>

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.650
<i>We're also having a star-viewing party!</i>

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:11.150
<i>There's so much to do!</i>

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.080
Hard at work, I see.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:17.740
Suzu-san.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:19.360
Huh? What's going on?

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.120
I ordered delivery from Suzuya Bakery.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.240
Suzuya Bakery is that popular bakery, right?

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.800
Yes! Suzu-chan's family runs it!

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:34.830
And their mascot, Moe-Moe, 
is Suzuya Moe-chan!

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.680
Please don't call me that.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.020
Suzu is her last name?

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:43.840
I don't like Moe-Moe, so I asked Mira 
to give me a new nickname.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.730
You called me Ino-senpai too, didn't you?

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.660
Yes! It's cute and easier to say!

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.550
Vice President, should we start 
calling her Ino-chan, too?

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.100
At this point?

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.510
Affectionately, you know?

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:02.940
Ino.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.200
Yes!

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:08.880
Isn't it kind of stiff to call each other 
President and Vice President, too?

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.820
Mira, why don't you give them nicknames?

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.490
Um, what are your full names?

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.020
Sakurai Mikage.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:21.220
Morino Mari.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.070
Then... Sakura-senpai and Monroe-senpai.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.730
Huh? Why?

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.040
Your name, your mole, and your breasts!

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.300
I get it.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.780
This bread is delicious!

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.450
It's my first time eating Suzuya's bread.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.480
It always sells out in the morning!

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.840
We're all sold out of our 
on-location stock, too!

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.120
I'm heading home, then.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.120
Huh? You're going already?

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.510
Suzu-san, the bread was very good!

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:54.920
I'll stop by your store sometime!

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.600
Sakura-senpai, this is good!

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:00.510
Thanks.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.670
Weren't you going back home?

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.950
Ino-senpai's so cute.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:44.520
02. The Riverside Milky Way

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.170
There really are so many kinds of stones.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.630
Sakura-senpai!

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:57.930
What kind of stone is this?

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.590
That's chert.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:03.700
It's a mineral formed from the deposits 
of shells shed by radiolarians and such.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.020
Wow!

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.590
And this one?

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.710
That's probably andesite.

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.020
It's made by volcanoes.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.010
And this?

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.380
Mudstone.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.520
It's a sedimentary rock 
made of very fine grains.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:16.700
And this?

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.600
That's dog poop!

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.480
Sakura-senpai, you saved my life.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.180
You're welcome.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.860
Oh, hey, that's a banded stone.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.680
A banded stone?

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.530
See the white band wrapping around it?

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.800
That part is a different mineral.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.810
They're known colloquially as banded stones.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.890
Some people use them 
as charms or talismans.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:40.070
Are you even listening?!

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:40.960
Huh?

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.330
Mira, did you find something?

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:44.720
Oh, look, look at this!

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:46.790
She said it's a banded stone.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:48.220
It's cute.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.060
I found one that looks like Jupiter.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.220
It does!

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.160
It even has the big red spot!

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.060
Good grief.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:00.000
You wanted to teach her even more, 
didn't you, Sakura-senpai?

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.180
They're both in the astronomy section, 
so teaching a little's just fine.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:08.120
Besides, it wouldn't sink in if I started 
with the hard stuff, anyway.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.990
Your body's honest, even if your words aren't.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.380
Senpai, what should we do with these stones?

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.230
First we'll take them back 
and clean them off.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:20.220
Then we'll categorize them as specimens...

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:26.080
What are you doing?

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.340
I had confidence in my legs, but...

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:32.380
You shouldn't go picking up 
anything and everything.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:34.730
But we went through the 
trouble gathering them.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:37.320
Goodbye, Banded Two.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.800
Mudstone Three, Chert Four.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:45.460
Rock collecting is full of 
once-in-a-lifetime encounters, huh?

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:46.180
Yes.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.220
The colors and shapes are all different,

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.660
and there are as many as there are stars.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.660
As many as there are stars...

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.140
Ao! This is incredible!

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.960
This river is like the Milky Way!

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.120
Huh? Oh, yeah.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:06.660
Well, the sun's going down, 
so it's time we cleaned up...

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:07.990
Ta-da!

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:10.010
Let's move on to part two!

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.860
It's a telescope!

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:20.470
Uh, do you know how to use a telescope?

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.190
S-Sorry.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:23.750
And you, Ao-san?

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:24.930
A little.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.400
Wow!

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.990
Could I ask you to focus it on Mars?

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:32.910
Huh? Oh. Uh...

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:36.950
Why don't I do it today?

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:45.470
There's a bit of a trick to focusing it.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:49.000
Focusing?

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.470
Bringing the star into view 
on the telescope.

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:54.600
First you peer through the finderscope,

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.150
and adjust the telescope until the star 
you want to view is dead center.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.480
Okay, you can see Mars now.

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.280
Ao, look!

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.180
Sure.

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.610
It's pretty.

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:14.250
Can I look?

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.900
Go ahead, go ahead.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:20.900
I can't see anything.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:24.080
Must be because of your bad behavior.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:25.000
Did you say something?

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.540
No, nothing.

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:30.520
It'll move out of view over time 
due to the Earth's rotation.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.240
It will?

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.960
Ao-san, want to try it?

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:36.050
Yes!

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:45.460
Here you go.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:46.820
Thanks.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.700
I see it!

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:54.260
You did it, Ao!

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:55.120
Yeah!

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:58.080
Mars really is red, isn't it?

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:01.630
But it's such a pain having 
to constantly adjust this.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:05.730
There is equipment that will automatically 
focus and follow them for you.

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:08.060
Why don't you buy it?

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:09.920
<b>Monthly Star Navigator</b>

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:09.920
You'd let us?

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:11.830
You can't?

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:16.360
It's 200,000 to 400,000 yen 
just for the telescope?

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.360
Never mind! You can't!

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:21.820
More stars are becoming 
visible by the moment.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:25.170
The one with two brighter stars is Gemini!

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:29.270
Over there, where you see 
the orangish star, is Boötes.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:30.730
How can you tell?

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:31.820
Huh?

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:33.110
Practice, I guess?

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:37.770
How can you tell different rocks apart?

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.410
Practice, I guess?

00:08:40.790 --> 00:08:44.910
That one that looks like an 
upside-down question mark is Leo.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:48.250
Endou-sensei, you're a Leo, right?

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:54.170
The second star past the
 Big Dipper is Spica, from Virgo.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.720
I'm a Virgo!

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.350
This is my first time seeing 
my own constellation!

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:03.970
Mira and Manaka-san are both Gemini, 
and Monroe-senpai is an Aquarius.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:04.780
Huh?

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:06.940
Suzu-chan's a Libra!

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.810
I never told you.

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:10.960
I'm a Cancer!

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:14.090
Cancer should be around that region,

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:16.160
but many of its stars are darker.

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:17.700
Huh? Really?

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:20.160
I don't think I've ever 
seen it properly, either.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:22.450
Aw...

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:24.420
Sakura-senpai.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.630
Come on! We should be 
able to see Jupiter soon!

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:31.500
I'm sure it's not much
 different than seeing Mars.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.670
You can see stripes and dots!

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.090
The dots around it are the Galilean Moons.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:41.150
You can see them pretty well, 
even with a smaller telescope.

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:44.430
Sakura-senpai, I want to see, too!

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:48.660
I'm glad they're enjoying it so much.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.150
Yeah, I'm happy, too.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.360
<i>Because you're happy.</i>

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:57.650
<i>I had to move far away after that day,</i>

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:00.320
<i>but I always wanted to see you again.</i>

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.110
<i>If we were to be reunited one day...</i>

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:06.360
I wanted to see so many more 
things together with you.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.540
Yeah, let's see lots of things!

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:11.980
Tonight the Lyrid meteor 
shower is at its peak.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.720
Really?! Will we be able 
to see shooting stars?

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:18.200
In addition, Vesta is close to Gemini.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:19.290
A vest?

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:20.930
No, Vesta.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:25.170
Vesta is an asteroid discovered 
around two hundred years ago.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:26.780
Gauss named it.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:28.620
<i>Das ist richtig.</i>

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:31.650
They've been discovering asteroids 
since that long ago?!

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:32.830
Yeah.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:36.880
At present, there are roughly 
500,000 known asteroids.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:38.650
F-Five hundred thousand?!

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:43.090
Since the world started using advanced 
telescopes to observe the skies,

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:46.330
most of the larger ones have 
been discovered already.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.480
I wonder if we'll be able to discover one.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:55.250
<i>Ao's smart, so I'm sure her worry 
is more specific than mine.</i>

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:58.520
Ao! I'll do my best!

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:01.590
I'll study a lot so I can catch up to you!

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:03.760
So let's do our best together!

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:07.600
<i>How could she try to catch up to me?</i>

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:11.850
<i>She's always the one in 
the lead, pulling me along.</i>

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:13.810
Yeah.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:14.990
All right!

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:19.380
First I'm going to hone my vision 
so I can find even small stars!

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:21.280
In that case,

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:25.760
you can test your vision with Mizar 
and Alcor in the Big Dipper.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.740
Oh, wow!

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.740
What are those two doing?

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.320
A spoon?

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.200
They're so close!

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:33.380
What was that for?!

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:35.800
Sorry for the flash.

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:39.840
I wanted to have photos for the report, 
so I've been taking some here and there.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:42.700
Hey! You've been taking them 
in secret this whole time!

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:44.300
These are...

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:45.490
I'll buy them!

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:46.260
Huh?!

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:51.030
D-Did you see that?!

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:51.700
Yes!

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:53.560
It's the Lyrid meteor shower.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:57.990
Only a few meteors appear every year, 
so you're kind of lucky to see one.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:11:59.100
Really?

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.320
When will the next one stream by?

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.240
What now? You saw a meteor?

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:06.010
Call me for that!

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:08.580
Where were you, Sensei?

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:10.660
In the car, because it's cold.

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:13.240
But tonight I'll tough it out!

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:14.620
Huh?

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:18.430
<i>There weren't any other meteors after that.</i>

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:31.430
<b>Sparkle

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:31.740
So, our first printing will 
be half-fold, four pages.

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:34.090
Each of us will have one column to write.

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:36.060
Has everyone decided on their topic?

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:40.090
I'm planning to write about the rocks 
we collected at the riverside.

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:41.900
I'll introduce Jupiter.

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:45.620
I'll use the photos I took to 
report what our club's done.

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:48.620
Photos? You mean...

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:50.130
I knew it!

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:53.410
What are you writing, Sakura-senpai?

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:55.370
I thought of a few different things,

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:57.540
but I figured this would be 
the most appealing to others.

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:05.300
<b>Birthstones
How to Enjoy Natural Rocks</b>

00:12:58.540 --> 00:12:59.920
Birthstones!

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:03.460
If you introduce them each in order, 
you'll be able to write twelve articles.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:06.010
You're naïve, Monroe.

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:08.430
There's a birthstone for 
every single day, so...

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:11.790
I can write 366 consecutive articles!

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.500
That's incredible, Senpai!

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.440
I see you fully intend 
to be held back a year.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:18.640
You're all incredible, having 
your topics figured out already.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:20.900
Sparkle

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:20.900
Topic

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:20.900
You're not writing about asteroids?

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:23.340
I thought about that...

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:26.550
but what if someone takes an interest 
in asteroids because of my article

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.960
and discovers one before we do?!

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:32.420
That would hurt! I'd regret it!

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:33.450
I don't think that'll be a problem.

00:13:33.450 --> 00:13:35.170
They'd have to be pretty hardcore.

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.060
I've decided on my topic for now, but...

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:39.910
Mira, you're doing homework?

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:43.340
No, I'm writing an article for my club.

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:45.050
Does it sound okay?

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:47.420
Well?

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:51.180
Onee-chan!

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:53.970
Sorry, it's just too explanatory.

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:54.790
Jeez!

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:57.880
It's supposed to explain what 
they are, so of course it is!

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.190
That's not what I mean.

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:01.360
I'm saying the article is too generic,

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.090
and I don't feel any aspect of you in it.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:09.900
Even our student council's report 
tweaks the text and the style a bit.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:16.280
You're saying so many
 things I don't understand!

00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:17.130
Mira!

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:27.900
You were at fault there.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.220
You asked her for advice,

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:31.230
and then you ran away halfway through.

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:36.100
But I worked so hard, and it felt 
like I was being dismissed.

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:38.890
Well, I'm not saying I don't sympathize.

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:42.990
When Misa-nee was the student council 
president, she acted sharp and stern,

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:45.710
but she was a surprisingly bad communicator.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:46.860
Though...

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:51.870
I accidentally made Mira upset...
How can I get her to forgive me?

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:51.870
Oh, my... How terrible.
What did Mira say?

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:49.960
Thanks to that, I've made 
progress chatting with Misa-nee,

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:51.870
which has been a godsend for me.

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:54.290
Suzu-chan, you're speaking out loud.

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:55.080
Oh?

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:58.550
I thought this would be easier to write.

00:14:58.550 --> 00:15:01.500
Well, it's like that for everyone 
the first time around.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.920
They wouldn't use the 
first bread I baked, either.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:07.130
Huh? It wasn't any good?

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:08.170
No.

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:10.360
They said it lacked dignity.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:12.290
They're panties.

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.510
Oh, it's from Ao.

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:21.650
"I'm looking for some illustrations 
to insert in the article."

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:23.010
My.

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:25.380
Well, then, why don't you draw 
some for a change of pace?

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:27.330
You always liked drawing, didn't you?

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:29.760
"I want an image that's Greek myth-ish

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:32.190
to introduce Jupiter and its moons," she said.

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:33.940
I see.

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:35.580
Greek myth-ish?

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:39.910
Yeah, there are a lot of connections 
between Greek myths and the stars.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:42.640
Sounds fun. Maybe I'll try reading up on them.

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:43.560
Done!

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:44.380
That was fast!

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:47.400
And sent!

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:51.610
If only I could write the 
article that quickly, too.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:54.470
That's it!

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:01.120
<i>Wow, Mira drew this for me!</i>

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:02.530
<i>It's cute!</i>

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:06.730
<i>"Thank you so much. 
Your illustration's very cute."</i>

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:07.740
<i>"No, thank you!"</i>

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:08.590
<i>"Thanks to you..."</i>

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:09.660
<i>"I found my answer!"</i>

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:10.700
<i>"Love, love!"</i>

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:12.650
<i>Huh?</i>

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:16.140
<b>Earth Sciences Club</b>

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:16.160
You made yours into a manga, Mira-chan?

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:17.760
Let's Enjoy Spring's Constellations

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:17.760
Nice to meet you. 
I'm the astrofox, Condorde.

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:17.760
I'm your space guide, Kururu! 
Nice to meet you.

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:17.760
I see you're fine without 
a space suit. Are you an alien?

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:17.760
Oh, no, not so
 much... Wait!

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:20.370
There might be less information 
than text would provide,

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.370
I don't want to hear 
that from a talking fox!

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.370
Setting that aside, let's introduce 
the spring constellations this time!

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.370
First, there's the spring triangle! When you follow the handle 
of the Big Dipper, you can 
find Arcturus in Boötes, 
and Spica in Virgo!

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.370
If you follow west from there, 
you find Leo. You'll recognize 
it by the head that looks like 
an upside-down question mark. 
The light blue first magnitude star 
at its heart, Regulus, is very pretty!

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.370
Nights are gentler this time 
of year, too, so it's easier 
to go look at the stars. You 
should all give it a try, too!

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.370
See you again~!

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:22.560
but it's interesting, so it's okay!

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:23.920
Hooray!

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:25.120
It's cute.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:25.920
Huh?

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:27.320
Ahem.

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:30.340
W-Well, I think having 
something like this is fine, too.

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:33.880
Now all that's left is the layout.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:35.950
Did we get that computer 
with our club budget?

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:39.370
No, we're borrowing 
Endou-sensei's personal computer.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:46.690
Incredible! It came together 
like a real report!

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:48.440
I think this turned out well, don't you?

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:49.270
Yes!

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:52.740
It's our very first issue of <i>Sparkle</i>.

00:16:58.100 --> 00:16:59.780
Do you think people will read it?

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:01.170
I'm nervous.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:03.730
Well, let's just be patient.

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:08.290
Um, do you have the earth 
sciences club newsletter here?

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:10.180
Oh, yes, here it is!

00:17:10.180 --> 00:17:11.250
I want one!

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:11.800
Me, too!

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:12.140
Give me one!

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:13.160
Me, too!

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:14.380
Huh?!

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:15.310
Here you go!

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:17.010
Hey, what's going on here?!

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:17.010
Yes, here you go!

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:19.460
The earth sciences club has 
printed their first issue

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:20.970
of their newsletter, <i>Sparkle</i>, today.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:24.100
If you're interested, please read it.

00:17:24.100 --> 00:17:26.230
They're being distributed 
in front of the faculty room.

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:28.140
O-Onee-chan!

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:29.060
Huh?!

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:31.500
You're the council president's little sister?!

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:35.200
<i>To celebrate the first printing of </i>Sparkle<i>,</i>

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:37.620
<i>we're having a little party at a day spa.</i>

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:41.800
This is my first time seeing you 
with your hair down, Ao.

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:44.220
Make sure you put it all up beforehand.

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:45.620
Oh, yes.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.780
You're surprisingly clumsy.

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:58.340
What are you looking at?

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.840
Oh, I was looking at the 
rocks and the paving stones.

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:04.390
We always get distracted when we see them.

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:07.520
It's like an occupational side effect, 
but from a school club.

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:09.680
You have your own in the 
astronomy group, too.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:10.500
Huh?

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:12.680
The moon!

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:16.440
Mira, everyone can see your butt.

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:19.360
I forgot it was a full moon tonight.

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:22.760
There's an elegance to viewing 
the moon from a bath!

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:28.620
Monroe-senpai.

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:30.090
You have no elegance.

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:30.960
Huh?

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:35.420
This bath water feels kind of slimy.

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:40.040
I saw the composition chart, 
and it said this was a chloride bath.

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:41.860
Uh, meaning?

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:44.250
There's salt dissolved in the bathwater.

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:47.800
The salt prevents sweat from evaporating,

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:49.680
so it's great for preserving heat.

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:52.340
It's great for stiff shoulders 
and those who get cold easily.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:54.890
You know a lot about hot springs, Ino-chan.

00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:57.620
I studied them a bit.

00:18:57.620 --> 00:19:00.800
There are a lot more 
types of hot springs, too.

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:04.850
For example, sulfur springs 
and acidic springs.

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:05.860
Acid?!

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:07.540
Wouldn't that hurt to bathe in?

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:11.720
I've heard that some stronger acidic 
springs can make eyes or wounds sting.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:15.240
The strongest one in Japan is pH 1.2.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:18.130
pH 1.2 is more acidic than lemons.

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:22.240
I never knew there were hot 
springs like this in the city.

00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:25.390
You often think of hot springs as being 
in the mountains or by the ocean.

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:30.120
The number of hot springs around cities 
and plains has been growing lately.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:34.390
Apparently, it counts as a hot spring if 
you dig up water that's over 25 degrees.

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:38.660
Temperatures go up three degrees 
every hundred meters deep you go,

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:41.860
so if you dig down a few hundred meters, 
you can get a hot spring here, too.

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:46.970
I wonder how this water 
built up underground.

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:51.760
Either rainwater seeped through 
the ground or it's fossil water.

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.050
Fossils?

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:56.390
It doesn't mean there are 
fossils in the water.

00:19:56.390 --> 00:20:00.130
It refers to ancient water 
trapped in deep strata.

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:05.930
Either way, it means it took a long time 
to get the water that's in our bath now.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:09.250
What are you doing?

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:11.480
Expressing my gratitude.

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:17.630
That was a great bath!

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:20.260
The best!

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:21.440
Yes!

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:25.570
My, we have a lot of youngsters here today.

00:20:25.850 --> 00:20:27.070
Good evening.

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:29.050
We're the Hoshizaki High 
School Earth Sciences Club.

00:20:29.050 --> 00:20:30.500
Good evening.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:34.790
Hoshizaki? Does that mean 
you're Yuki-chan's students?

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:36.020
Yuki-chan?

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:38.540
Do you mean Endou-sensei?

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:40.760
You know our teacher?

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:44.310
She's been a regular customer 
here for many years.

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:48.130
Hey, are there any nice men 
for Yuki-chan at your school?

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:49.700
Hey, Take-san.

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:52.580
You've been worried about 
her too, though, Matsu-san.

00:20:52.580 --> 00:20:53.810
Well, sure.

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:57.270
Yuki-chan's always been such 
a diligent, talented girl.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:59.150
Diligent?

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:00.440
Talented?

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:02.990
When she first started teaching,

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:06.190
she had so many worries 
that she let herself go a lot.

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:10.750
She used to say our hot springs and 
her club were a great breather for her.

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:13.560
Oh, my, we'll make Yuki-chan mad at us

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:15.420
if we keep sharing so much 
with her students.

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:19.280
<i>You're saying that after spilling it all.</i>

00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:23.240
Being a teacher must be hard.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:26.660
We'll have to be nicer to her from now on.

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:29.350
Sensei!

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:31.140
Hey.

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:33.400
Thank you for the ticket book!

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:35.130
We already went and used them!

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:37.440
They were pretty great baths, weren't they?

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:38.940
Here, a souvenir!

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:39.760
Huh?

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:41.740
Oh, thank you.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:44.000
Sensei, please keep at it!

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:45.320
Huh?

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:50.830
Huh?

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:54.930
<i>Our activities in the earth 
sciences club have just begun,</i>

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:58.650
<i>and our dream of discovering 
an asteroid is a long, long way off,</i>

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:03.760
<i>but I'm sure a sparkling world awaits us!</i>

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:40.730
03.
Memories Are Treasures

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:39.100
Next time, "Memories Are Treasures."
